          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Where does guidance differ on non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated UTI?",
          "answer": "Guidance on the non-antibiotic management of uncomplicated urinary tract infections (UTIs) differs among various health organizations. Some European guidelines suggest offering symptomatic treatment with delayed or no antibiotics as a shared decision, based on evidence that some episodes resolve without antibiotic treatment. In contrast, other guidelines still recommend immediate antibiotics as the default option, citing concerns about symptom duration and the potential risk of progression to more severe kidney involvement.\n\nThe disagreement revolves around the balance of avoiding unnecessary antibiotic exposure versus the need to manage symptoms effectively. The discussion is not about the facts surrounding UTIs but about the value of limiting antibiotic use in certain cases (source: Infectious diseases and urology society guidance).\n\nI could not cover specific clinical practices or detailed recommendations from various health organizations regarding the management of uncomplicated UTIs beyond this overview.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
